y that column for  example and then i can arrange  by something if i were to write this out  in sql  i would have to um i would start with  yeah the table  the condition group by now the select or  the summarize  jumps all the way up to the top um  having is at the bottom again order by  is also at the bottom  and so you have to kind of remember that  the ordering is a little bit different  you don't necessarily  write things in the order that you would  do them um  but other than that but it's something  that you you get used to  fairly quickly um and if you're like me  and  and you spend a lot of time in our  studio the nice thing is there are a lot  of sql things  you can do from within that familiar  environment of our  and especially with the newer versions  of the ide  for example if you open up a sql file  you have your nice  you know colors syntax highlighting so  you can see what the verbs are what the  you know the table names are  what i didn't realize for the longest  time is there's this line at the top  that says you know exclamation mark  preview with some  stuff after that this is where you can  actually set a connection  so if you set a connection to the  database you can get the sql preview  pane  where you can preview that sql query  within rstudio  this took me a while to figure out but  it's a really nice feature  our markdown is this kind of magical  document where you can mix  all kinds of different languages and r  and sql is no exception  you'll start probably with some kind of  animal header like usual  you might have an r setup chunk where  you have packages  the database connection maybe you'll set  up a table like i did here  and then you can have your sql query and  if you have this inline  mode uh enabled you can actually see  those  previews within our markdown and work  with both r  and sql interactively within this one  document  and finally you can navigate through  your database  from within r you can see what databases  you have 